. Giufre M, Cardines R, Caporali MG, Accogli M, D’Ancona F, et al. (2011) Ten years of Hib vaccination in Italy: prevalence of non-encapsulated Haemophilus influenzae among invasive isolates and the possible impact on antibiotic resistance. Vaccine 29: 3857–3862. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 35. Ladhani S, Heath PT, Ramsay ME, Slack MP (2008) Changes in antibiotic resistance rates of invasive Haemophilus influenzae isolates in England and Wales over the last 20 years. J Antimicrob Chemother 62: 776–779. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 36. Shuel M, Law D, Skinner S, Wylie J, Karlowsky J, et al. (2010) Characterization of nontypeable Haemophilus influenzae collected from respiratory infections and invasive disease cases in Manitoba, Canada. FEMS Immunol Med Microbiol 58: 277–284. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 37. Shuel M, Hoang L, Law DK, Tsang R (2011) Invasive Haemophilus influenzae in British Columbia: non-Hib and non-typeable strains causing disease in children and adults. Int J Infect Dis 15: e167–e173. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 38. Sill ML, Law DK, Zhou J, Skinner S, Wylie J, et al. (2007) Population genetics and antibiotic susceptibility of invasive Haemophilus influenzae in Manitoba, Canada, from 2000 to 2006. FEMS Immunol Med Microbiol 51: 270–276. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 39. Jansen WT, Verel A, Beitsma M, Verhoef J, Milatovic D (2006) Longitudinal European surveillance study of antibiotic resistance of Haemophilus influenzae . J Antimicrob Chemother 58: 873–877. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 40. Shuel ML, Tsang RS (2009) Canadian beta-lactamase-negative Haemophilus influenzae isolates showing decreased susceptibility toward ampicillin have significant penicillin binding protein 3 mutations. Diagn Microbiol Infect Dis 63: 379–383. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Data Availability Statement The authors confirm that all data underlying the findings are fully available without restriction. All relevant data are within the paper.',117,'2025-10-20 06:28:25','2025-10-20 06:28:25');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(6916,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11824084','Attitudes of members of the German Society for Palliative Medicine toward complementary and alternative medicine for cancer patients.','Conrad AC, Muenstedt K, Micke O, Prott FJ, Muecke R, Huebner J.','2014','magazines eschiti mcgrail saxe muenstedt naturopaths sollner micke specter mskcc mcgrail dgp dgp prio menus thanner dkg unipark questback hospices dgp hospices disapproval bioresonance mushrooms massages aroma trimborn downer heese dgp gilmour hann micke muenstedt boddy trimborn discloses sensitize molassiotis micke huebner hitlist dgp dgp ostgathe dgp boddy geoghegan downer cody mccluskey arnott lister slevin eschiti gilmour asadi s187 s192 hann denniston entrekin heese goldbrunner tonn huebner muecke muenstedt micke kritische internetseiten biologische krebsabwehr onkologe micke bruns glatzel schonekaes micke buntzel micke buntzel kisters micke vasey molassiotis fernadez pud ozden panteli muenstedt entezami wartenberg kullmer uct muenstedt harren georgi nen079 greisinger singletary mcgrail saxe madlensky kealey sollner maislinger devries steixner rumpold thanner geld glaube warum schulmediziner heilverfahren anbieten sertel stassen rapp walburg wege alternativmedizin fluch segen trimborn senf muenstedt buentzel micke muecke prott wicker huebner muenstedt schweder senf ruckhaeberle huebner','Abstract Background A high proportion of cancer patients use complementary and alternative medicine (CAM). In oncology, risks of CAM are side effects and interactions. Objective Our aim was to conduct a survey on professionals in palliative care regarding attitudes toward CAM. Design and subjects An internet-based survey with a standardized questionnaire was sent to all members of the German Society for Palliative Care. Measurements The questionnaire collected data on attitude toward CAM and experiences. Results Six hundred and ninety questionnaires (19 %) were returned (49 % physicians, 35 % nurses, 3 % psychologists). Acceptance of CAM is high (92 % for complementary and 54 % for alternative medicine). Most participants had already been asked on CAM by patients (95 %) and relatives (89 %). Forty-four percent already had used complementary methods and 5 % alternative methods. Only 21 % think themselves adequately informed. Seventy-four percent would use complementary methods in a patient with advanced tumor, and 62 % would use alternative therapy in patients if there was no other therapy. Even from those who are skeptical 45 % would treat a patient with alternative methods. Conclusions In order to inform patients on CAM and to further patients’ autonomy, evidence on benefits and harms of CAM must be provided. As awareness of risks from CAM is low and critical appraisal especially of alternative medicine missing, but interest on information on CAM is high, experts should provide evidence-based recommendations for CAM in palliative care to members of different professions. This could be done by a curriculum focusing on the most often used CAM methods. Electronic supplementary material The online version of this article (doi:10.1007/s00432-014-1656-9) contains supplementary material, which is available to authorized users. Keywords: Complementary medicine, Alternative medicine, Palliative care, Cancer, Attitude of professionals Introduction Cancer patients have high interest in complementary and alternative medicine (CAM). About 40–50 % of all patients use CAM, in some entities like breast cancer up to 90 % has been reported. Typical users are female and have a higher level of education. Primary sources of information on CAM are family members and friends, less frequently magazines and books and rarely physicians and the oncologist (Eschiti 2007 ). Most patients do not even inform their oncologists about the CAM methods they use (Robinson and McGrail 2004 ; Saxe et al. 2008 ). In contrast to data from other western countries, in Germany, the most common source for CAM recommendations are physicians (Muenstedt et al. 2000 ) mostly general practitioners and naturopaths. Several studies report that patients with advanced cancer use CAM more often than others (Sollner et al. 2000 ; Micke et al. 2009 , 2010 ). Yet so far, no data on attitudes and behavior of patients in palliative care setting are available. Complementary and alternative medicine in oncology is a particularly sensitive issue since side effects and interactions can induce adverse events. As most oncologists are not informed on their patients using CAM, they will not be able to regard side effects and interactions with CAM substances as reason for adverse effects they diagnose in their patients. Only few studies report on possible interactions and mostly only case reports deal with side effects. Yet, an analysis of possible interactions of outpatients from a certified breast center in Germany points to a high rate of probable and possible interactions (Zeller et al. 2013 ). Adverse effects may go unnoticed as in oncology most drugs have a broad specter of side effects on different organs. Additionally, especially in a palliative setting, patients may focus on alternative treatments and omit conventional treatments. With disease progressing, this may also lead to replacement of substantial palliative care. So far, no reliable data on this issue have been published. Whereas we have some data on interactions of CAM and chemotherapy, only few data are available on interaction of CAM and drugs used in palliative care (Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center: About Herbs: http://www.mskcc.org/cancer-care/integrative-medicine/about-herbs ). In order to prevent these interactions, communication between patients and physicians is mandatory. There are several reasons why patients do not inform their physicians. Mostly they do not think him or her might be competent or interested in this field. Others are afraid of being told to stop CAM or even have tried to talk about CAM, but did not get a respectful answer (Robinson and McGrail 2004 ). A decisive element in patient–physician communication on CAM thus is the attitude of physicians (and other professionals) toward CAM and their knowledge regarding the different methods. The aim of the present study was to conduct a survey on CAM in palliative care in a representative group of professionals in Germany. This survey should focus on interest in and attitude toward CAM as well as willingness to use CAM. To our knowledge, this is the first survey on this topic with professionals in palliative care. Methods For our study, we decided to use the following differentiation: Complementary medicine is accompanying therapy and is fine-tuned to conventional therapy, and alternative therapy is applied instead of conventional therapy (Cassileth 2012 ). This definition was also part of the introduction to the questionnaire. Study population After a pretest (see below), all members of the German Society for Palliative Care (DGP) were asked via email to participate in an internet-based survey. This survey was open for 3 months in 2011. The access was only possible with the link send in the email. Participation in the survey was voluntary and anonymous. Questionnaire (see also supplemental material) The questionnaire was developed in cooperation of the working group Complementary Medicine and the working group Clinical Research of the DGP and the working group Prevention and Integrative Oncology (PRIO) of the German Cancer Society. The questionnaire included three sections: Personal data Personal attitude toward CAM and own experiences Professional attitudes toward CAM Most questions were closed questions using either yes/no or rating on a Likert scale or drop down menus. In “ Introduction ” section, we assessed gender, age, state the person was born in, state of Germany the person lives, field of work (palliative ward, stationary hospice, palliative counseling service…), profession and specialty (if applicable). In “ Methods ” section, we asked participants on their private attitude toward and experience with CAM. Two questions focused on own experience with CAM and experience in family members or friends. In an open question, we asked participants to explicitly name examples for positive and negative experiences. All questions in this section were distinguishing complementary from alternative medicine. Finally, we asked whether the participants would use complementary and/or alternative therapies in case of advanced cancer without other active therapy against the tumor for themselves, relatives or a near friend. In “ Results ” section, a set of questions assessed interest in CAM, source of information and need for information. We proceeded asking whether the participants had been asked on CAM by patients or relatives before and whether they already had used CAM in their patients. Then we turned to the setting of palliative care for cancer patients. We asked who should be responsible for CAM and whether CAM should be paid for by statutory health insurance. In the second part of this section, we asked whether the participants would use alternative therapies in a patient with advanced cancer (unlimited, if there is no other therapy against cancer and the patient has a desire to be treated, additionally to conventional therapy in a patient with advanced tumor without limitations). If participants did agree to any of these proposals, they were additionally asked which criteria they would accept for a choice on CAM (I would accept any recommendation, as long as the patient feels, that this will alleviate his symptoms. I would fully inform myself on the therapy chosen by the patient and then make a decision). Next, we presented a list of arguments for or against CAM and asked the participants about their consent. These reasons correspond to the most frequently cited arguments by physicians (Thanner 2010 ) (These methods maybe do not help but they neither can do any harm, … have positive effects on palliative patients with cancer, … can make the patient less compliant with important recommendations regarding therapy, … may help the patient to better cope with his disease, As science does not know much on these methods, I do not recommend them. These methods can have negative interactions with drugs from palliative care). In the following part, we provided a list of different methods from CAM in which the participants should mark whether or not they recommend this method as complementary or alternative therapy. This list of complementary methods and substances which are currently used most frequently by patients with tumors corresponds to the list contained in a questionnaire for patients prepared by the working group “Prevention and Integrative Oncology” of the German Cancer Society (DKG), which is currently used in a number of studies designed to analyze user behavior. The list is based on published surveys on user behavior in Germany and on the palliative care experts’ own practical experience gathered over many years of patient counseling. The questionnaire was programmed as an online questionnaire (using Unipark/Questback) and sent to the participants as a link. Pretest The first version of the questionnaire was tested on employees of 4 palliative care wards, 2 hospices and a hospice group and 3 specialized palliative care teams. Sixty-eight individuals followed the link and 24 individuals (35 %) completed the questionnaire as a whole, 12 (18 %) only looked at the first page and a further 11 (16 %) stopped while filling in demographic data. Therefore, we revised the questionnaire by changing the order of the questions and adapting introduction and layout. Statistics Analysis of frequencies and cross tables with chi-square tests were done using IBM SPSS Statistics 20. We analyzed only those questionnaires which contained full information on profession and gender. Results At the time of the survey, the DGP had about 3,740 members and 690 questionnaires (18.5 %) were returned, of which 537 were filled in with complete personal data and thus were included into our analysis. 33.7 % of the participants worked in a palliative care unit, 20.1 % in specialized palliative care teams for outpatients, 9.7 % as palliative consultants in clinics, 8.0 % in hospices and 10.1 % in general palliative care for outpatients. 18.1 % came from other institutions. 36.7 % of participants were male and 63.3 % female. Age ranged from 24 to 87 years with a mean of 47 years. Regarding profession, 49.2 % were physicians, 34.8 % nurses, 3.2 % psychologists, 3.0 % physiotherapists and 1.5 % ministers or volunteers, respectively. 23.1 % of physicians had a specialization in palliative care. Acceptance of complementary medicine in palliative care is high (92.2 %) while for alternative therapies, disapproval is more prominent (40.6 %) (Figs. 1 , 2 ). Yet, alternative medicine also is accepted by 53.5, 28.5 % of whom fully accept it. There is a significant difference between male and female participants, with females accepting complementary medicine more often than males. In a comparison of physicians ( n = 264) and nurses ( n = 187), the former more often object to complementary therapies (5.3 vs. 0.5 %; p < 0.05) and even more to alternative therapies (53.7 vs. 26.8 %; p < 0.05). Female physicians tend to accept CAM more often than male (91.2 vs. 80.6 %; n.s.) while there is no difference between male and female nurses. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Acceptance of complementary medicine in different professions Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Acceptance of alternative medicine in different professions Five hundred and four of the participants (93.9 %) stated that they already had had experience with CAM in the private setting (Fig. 3 ). Fig. 3. Open in a new tab Private experiences of the participants with CAM 71.9 % of participants would use CAM in case of advanced cancer if they themselves or a member of the family were affected. Turning to the professional setting most participants had already been asked on CAM by patients (94.8 %) and relatives (89.2 %). 43.9 % already had used complementary methods and 5.2 % alternative methods, only 20.7 % never had used CAM. 85.1 % of all participants were interested in CAM. Only 20.9 % think themselves adequately informed and 77.3 % would like to get more information. 71.9 % already use at least one source of information. These sources are scientific journals in 53.6 %, educational in 39.7 %, the internet in 36.1 % and physicians with expertise in 32.6 %. Concerning interest and source of information we did not find any difference between physicians and nurses. Concerning the question who should be responsible for providing information on CAM to patients, the qualified expert of naturopathy is most frequently mentioned by all participants (25 %) (Fig. 4 ). Fig. 4. Open in a new tab Perception of responsibility for providing information on CAM to patients 74.3 % would use complementary methods in a patient with advanced tumor, 62.0 % would use alternative therapy in a patient if there was no other therapy against cancer and the patient had a desire to be treated and 32.1 % would use alternative therapies in a patient with advanced cancer unlimited. Even from those who are skeptical or opposed toward alternative medicine, 45 % would treat a patient in an advanced situation with alternative methods (Fig. 5 ). They would rather inform themselves thoroughly (89.5 %) as accept any method (10.5 %), whereas nurses would accept patient’s proposals in 31.5 % and look for information in 68.5 %. The answers to different reasons for or against CAM are shown in Fig. 6 . Fig. 5. Open in a new tab Willingness to use CAM in case of advanced cancer Fig. 6. Open in a new tab Reasons for or against CAM In the last section of the questionnaire, we asked the participants to declare which methods from a list of CAM methods they use on patients (in a complementary or alternative way or both) and which ones they do not recommend. The results are presented in Fig. 7 . Physicians most often recommend relaxation techniques (91 %), exercise therapy (84 %), spiritual care and prayer (82 %), sound and music therapy (80 %) and art therapy (79 %). They advice against diets (53 %), hyperthermia (42 %), magnetic field therapy (41 %), bioresonance (37 %), vitamins (35 %) and medical mushrooms (35 %). In nurses, positive recommendations are more frequent. Basal stimulation is recommended by 89 %. Other frequently recommended therapies are massages (88 %), aroma therapy (79 %) and homeopathy (63 %). Fig. 7. Open in a new tab Recommendations for different methods (number in brackets give the number of participants who knew the method and rated it) Discussion To our knowledge, this is the first survey concerning CAM in professionals working in palliative care. As our survey demonstrates, CAM is of high relevance in palliative care. Most participants have already been asked about CAM by patients or relatives and most would use CAM. In general, acceptance of complementary medicine is very high (more than 90 %). Furthermore, 80 % of all participants believe that patients have a benefit from using CAM. There is a significant difference between male and female participants, females accepting CAM more often than males. This is in line with our data from professionals of a university hospital (Trimborn et al. 2013 ) (and with greater interest in the topic in female patients (Downer et al. 1994 ; Richardson et al. 2000 ; Miller et al. 2009 ; Heese et al. 2010 ). A third of participants think that CAM will do no harm and more than 60 % are convinced that interactions with drugs from palliative care most probably are no problem. Only the small group of oncologists ( n = 36) 78 % regards interactions as relevant problem in palliative care. Also, a third of all participants fully accept using alternative therapies (according to our definition) on cancer patients in palliative setting without limitations. One limitation of our study is that we did not validate our questionnaire. Yet, as far as we know, no validated questionnaire on CAM has been published. External validated evidence is missing with which to compare data acquired using our questionnaire. As the topic CAM is highly controversial, interviews, e.g., will not yield reliable data as well. Yet, a preliminary internal validation using a factorial analysis would have been possible. Another limitation of our study is the small number participants due to which we cannot say whether the sample is a representative sample of the members of the DGP or not. Our questionnaire was sent to all members of the German Society for Palliative Care which is the only scientific society in this field in Germany. Yet, we do not have an access to professionals who are not members and might be different from those who are. Furthermore, it might be possible, that only those, who are interested in the topic, filled in the questionnaire. In this case, it seems probable that more members took part who favor CAM than those who are critical. In summary, risks from CAM are rarely perceived and a critical appraisal, especially of alternative medicine seems to be missing in a large group of professionals engaged in palliative care. Yet, this critical appraisal (apart from above mentioned possible adverse effects) seems mandatory considering treatments with lack of evidence. One explanation could be that professionals attending palliative patients are likely to accept patients’ desires as most important argument for or against a treatment. Yet, this focus on patients’ needs and preferences should not interfere with complete and honest information on benefits and risks of any treatment, the patient wants to use. From an ethical point of view, balanced and comprehensive information on any treatment is mandatory—this also refers to CAM (Gilmour et al. 2011 ). Considering benefit-burden ratio in complementary and alternative medicine in palliative care several aspects are important. First of all, benefit might be composed of positive effects on symptoms but also psychological effects. In accordance with these data from the literature, nearly 80 % of participants are convinced that “these methods help patients to cope with their disease.” As for complementary treatments, evidence regarding anticancer or supportive effects is missing (otherwise they would be part of conventional treatments), and the real benefit for patients might be that complementary therapies may support coping. Second, regarding harm, different aspects have to be considered: For side effects and interactions from CAM, we mostly lack data, yet, this does not exclude problems but is due to missing systematic assessment of this topic in modern medicine. In fact, side effects of biological-based CAM treatments as reported in case reports are similar to side effects of cancer treatments (e.g., hepatotoxicity or nephrotoxicity) and may go unnoticed as well as interactions if the physician is not aware of the patient using this complementary substance. Furthermore, burden for patient also refers to interventions such as injections, etc. as well as time of financial burden which the patient accepts because he hopes for positive effects. From an ethical point of view, counseling on CAM is a crucial topic in shared decision-making. Considering CAM as methods, which further patients’ autonomy (Davidson et al. 2005 ; Hann et al. 2005 ; Micke et al. 2009 ), patients should get all information on benefit and harm in order to make their decisions. This information can only be derived from evidence in sense of evidence-based medicine. One of the biggest problems, patients looking for information on CAM are facing, is that their doctors are not informed. In published surveys, most physicians and even more nurses think that they are not sufficiently informed (Frenkel et al. 2010 , Muenstedt et al. 2011 ). This also is due to the fact that it is difficult to gather reliable information even for physicians (Boddy and Ernst 2008 ). The problem we have to solve urgently is how to provide evidence-based recommendations in the specific setting of palliative care to members of different professions. Some clues to this strategy can be derived from our survey. Most professionals admit not being informed on CAM but are interested in the topic. They already use sources of information as scientific journals, courses and the internet. Yet, physicians are much more likely to check for evidence than nurses as in general in our survey, physicians are more skeptical toward CAM than nurses (this is in line with our data from of a German university hospital (Trimborn et al. 2013 ). Nevertheless, our survey discloses a high proportion of caregivers in palliative care favoring complementary and even alternative medicine without regarding lack of evidence. A first step could be to sensitize professionals on possible risks of CAM. At the same time, we should provide reliable data on CAM for palliative care. This collection of data can be concise as we know that for decades there is a stable user behavior at least concerning cancer patients (Molassiotis et al. 2005 , Micke et al. 2009 , Huebner et al. 2012 ; Zeller et al. 2013 ). A curriculum could be built on this “hitlist” and would be sufficient to provide knowledge for most discussions with patients. Qualifying members from different professions of interdisciplinary teams in CAM would allow for counseling palliative cancer patients. The question who should be in charge of providing this information then could be answered with respect to this qualification. Electronic supplementary material Below is the link to the electronic supplementary material. Supplementary material 1 (DOCX 24 kb) (24.3KB, docx) Acknowledgements The authors thank the German Society for Palliative Medicine (DGP) and the Working Group Research of the DGP, especially Prof. Dr. Ostgathe; University Erlangen, for their contribution to the conception of the questionnaire and help with providing the questionnaire to the members of the DGP. Conflict of interest The authors declare not to have any conflict of interests. References Boddy K, Ernst E (2008) Review of reliable information sources related to integrative oncology. Hematol Oncol Clin North Am 22: 619–30, vii [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Cassileth BR (2012) Alternative therapies: learning from patients who choose them. Oncologist 17:590–591 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Davidson R, Geoghegan L, McLaughlin L (2005) Psychological characteristics of cancer patients who use complementary therapies. Psychooncology 14:187–195 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Downer SM, Cody MM, McCluskey P, Wilson PD, Arnott SJ, Lister TA, Slevin ML (1994) Pursuit and practice of complementary therapies by cancer patients receiving conventional treatment. BMJ 309(6947):86–89 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Eschiti VS (2007) Lesson from comparison of CAM use by women with female-specific cancers to others: it‘s time to focus on interaction risks with CAM therapies. Integr Cancer Ther 6:313–344 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Frenkel M, Ben-Arye E, Cohen L (2010) Communication in cancer care: discussing complementary and alternative medicine. Integr Cancer Ther 9:177–185 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Gilmour J, Harrison C, Asadi L, Cohen MH, Vohra S (2011) Informed consent: advising patients and parents about complementary and alternative medicine therapies. Pediatrics 128(Suppl 4):S187–S192 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Hann D, Baker F, Denniston M, Entrekin N (2005) Long-term breast cancer survivors’ use of complementary therapies: perceived impact on recovery and prevention of recurrence. Integr Cancer Ther 4:14–20 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Heese O, Schmidt M, Nickel S, Berger H, Goldbrunner R, Tonn JC (2010) Complementary therapy use in patients with glioma: an observational study. Long-term breast cancer survivors’ use of complementary therapies. Neurology 75:2229–2235 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Huebner J, Muecke R, Muenstedt K, Micke O (2012) Kritische analyse der Internetseiten der Gesellschaft für Biologische Krebsabwehr. Onkologe 18:908–914 [ Google Scholar ] Micke O, Bruns F, Glatzel M, Schönekaes K, Micke P, Mücke R, Büntzel J (2009) Predictive factors for the use of complementary and alternative medicine (CAM) in radiation oncology. Eur J Integr Med 1:22–30 [ Google Scholar ] Micke O, Buntzel J, Kisters K, Schafer U, Micke P, Mucke R (2010) Complementary and alternative medicine in lung cancer patients: a neglected phenomenon? Front Radiat Ther Oncol 42:198–205 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Miller PE, Vasey JJ, Short PF, Hartman TJ (2009) Dietary supplement use in adult cancer survivors. Oncol Nurs Forum 36:61–68 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Molassiotis A, Fernadez-Ortega P, Pud D, Ozden G, Scott JA, Panteli V (2005) Use of complementary and alternative medicine in cancer patients: a European survey. Ann Oncol 16:655–663 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Muenstedt K, Entezami A, Wartenberg A, Kullmer U (2000) The attitudes of physicians and oncologists towards unconventional cancer therapies (UCT). Eur J Cancer 36:2090–2095 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Muenstedt K, Harren H, von Georgi R (2011) Complementary and Alternative Medicine: comparison of current knowledge, attitudes and interest among German medical students and doctors. Evid Based Complement Alternative Med. doi:10.1093/ecam/nen079 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] Richardson MA, Sanders T, Palmer JL, Greisinger A, Singletary SE (2000) Complementary/alternative medicine use in a comprehensive cancer center and the implications for oncology. J Clin Oncol 18:2505–2514 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Robinson A, McGrail MR (2004) Disclosure of CAM use to medical practitioners: a review of qualitative and quantitative studies. Complement Ther Med 12:90–98 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Saxe GA, Madlensky L, Kealey S, Wu DP, Freeman KL, Pierce JP (2008) Disclosure to physicians of CAM use by breast cancer patients: findings from the women‘s healthy eating and living study. Integr Cancer Ther 7:122–127 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Sollner W, Maislinger S, DeVries A, Steixner E, Rumpold G, Lukas P (2000) Use of complementary and alternative medicine by cancer patients is not associated with perceived distress or poor compliance with standard treatment but with active coping behavior: a survey. Cancer 89:873–880 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Thanner M (2010) Geld oder Glaube? Warum Schulmediziner alternative Heilverfahren anbieten. In: Becker R, Sertel S, Stassen-Rapp I, Walburg I (Hg.) „Neue“Wege in der Medizin: Alternativmedizin – Fluch oder Segen? Heidelberg Trimborn A, Senf B, Muenstedt K, Buentzel J, Micke O, Muecke R, Prott FJ, Wicker S, Huebner J (2013) Attitude of employees of a university clinic to complementary and alternative medicine in oncology. Ann Oncol 24:2641–2645 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Zeller T, Muenstedt K, Stoll C, Schweder J, Senf B, Ruckhaeberle E, Serve H, Huebner J (2013) Potential interactions of complementary and alternative medicine with cancer therapy in outpatients with gynecological cancer in a comprehensive cancer center. J Cancer Res Clin Oncol 139:357–365 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplementary material 1 (DOCX 24 kb) (24.3KB, docx)',11,'2025-10-20 06:28:33','2025-10-20 06:28:33');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(6917,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1716152','Behavioral genetics ''97: ASHG statement. Recent developments in human behavioral genetics: past accomplishments and future directions.','Sherman SL, DeFries JC, Gottesman II, Loehlin JC, Meyer JM, Pelias MZ, Rice J, Waldman I.','1997','deviant dissect celebrate antonarakis blouin pulver wolyniec lasseter nestadt kasch babb kazazian dombroski 6p24 ng1195 boomsma molenaar bf01065991 eaves bf01065484 floderus myrhed rasmuson eysenck bf01066265 gottesman bertelsen unexpressed fraternal gurling kalsi 6p24 ng1195 hanis boerwinkle ellsworth concannon wapelhorst spielman gogolin ewens ng0696 hugot gower beaugerie naom dupas gossum orholm 379821a0 jinks fulker biometrical genetical mava h0029135 gruenberg adoptees kety wender ingraham schizophrenic adoptees kruglyak multipoint langinvainio kaprio koskenvuo lonnqvist reared gemellol s0001566000007303 altstiel haroutunian purohit birstein lantz gepi5 eaves genetical hdy mccartney bernieri moises kristbjarnarson byerley macciardi arolt blackwood ng1195 mowry nancarrow lennon sandkuijl siever endicott 6p24 ng1195 plomin mcclearn reared reared pelias pennington dissect roses nm0396 albus hallmayer honig borrmann lichtermann ebstein ackenheil lerer risch multipoint ng1195 straub shinkwin 6p24 ng1195 tellegen lykken reared tienari tb03178 viken kaprio koskenvuo','Abstract The field of behavioral genetics has enormous potential to uncover both genetic and environmental influences on normal and deviant behavior. Behavioral-genetic methods are based on a solid foundation of theories and methods that successfully have delineated components of complex traits in plants and animals. New resources are now available to dissect the genetic component of these complex traits. As specific genes are identified, we can begin to explore how these interact with environmental factors in development. How we interpret such findings, how we ask new questions, how we celebrate the knowledge, and how we use or misuse this knowledge are all important considerations. These issues are pervasive in all areas of human research, and they are especially salient in human behavioral genetics. Full text PDF Previous 1265 1266 1267 1268 1269 … 1275 Next Selected References These references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article. Antonarakis S. E., Blouin J. L., Pulver A. E., Wolyniec P., Lasseter V. K., Nestadt G., Kasch L., Babb R., Kazazian H. H., Dombroski B. Schizophrenia susceptibility and chromosome 6p24-22. Nat Genet. 1995 Nov;11(3):235–236. doi: 10.1038/ng1195-235. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Boomsma D. I., Molenaar P. C. The genetic analysis of repeated measures. I. Simplex models. Behav Genet. 1987 Mar;17(2):111–123. doi: 10.1007/BF01065991. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Eaves L. J., Long J., Heath A. C. A theory of developmental change in quantitative phenotypes applied to cognitive development. Behav Genet. 1986 Jan;16(1):143–162. doi: 10.1007/BF01065484. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Floderus-Myrhed B., Pedersen N., Rasmuson I. Assessment of heritability for personality, based on a short-form of the Eysenck Personality Inventory: a study of 12,898 twin pairs. Behav Genet. 1980 Mar;10(2):153–162. doi: 10.1007/BF01066265. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Gottesman I. I., Bertelsen A. Confirming unexpressed genotypes for schizophrenia. Risks in the offspring of Fischer''s Danish identical and fraternal discordant twins. Arch Gen Psychiatry. 1989 Oct;46(10):867–872. doi: 10.1001/archpsyc.1989.01810100009002. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Gurling H., Kalsi G., Chen A. C., Green M., Butler R., Read T., Murphy P., Curtis D., Sharma T., Chen A. H. Schizophrenia susceptibility and chromosome 6p24-22. Nat Genet. 1995 Nov;11(3):234–235. doi: 10.1038/ng1195-234. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Hanis C. L., Boerwinkle E., Chakraborty R., Ellsworth D. L., Con